WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.693
Background services often encounter duplicate messages, retries, delayed processing, partial failures, and interrupted operations.

2
00:00:08.693 --> 00:00:11.970
A queue may deliver the same work more than once.

3
00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:16.116
A database update may succeed while a notification call fails.

4
00:00:16.116 --> 00:00:21.132
A worker may stop after writing a file but before marking the job complete.

5
00:00:21.132 --> 00:00:25.077
Secure and reliable design assumes these conditions happen.

6
00:00:25.077 --> 00:00:32.032
Idempotency means designing an operation so repeated processing does not cause unsafe duplicate effects.

7
00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:43.400
An ExampleJobId, idempotency key, state machine, unique constraint, or processed-record marker can help the worker determine whether the intended change already happened.

8
00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:46.410
The goal is not only to avoid duplicate work.

9
00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:54.167
It is to avoid duplicate state transitions, duplicate exports, duplicate notifications, and confusing audit records.

10
00:00:54.167 --> 00:00:56.708
Retry policies need limits and review.

11
00:00:56.708 --> 00:01:00.587
Retrying a temporary dependency failure may be reasonable.

12
00:01:00.587 --> 00:01:06.003
Retrying a permanently invalid message wastes resources and hides the real issue.

13
00:01:06.003 --> 00:01:13.359
Retrying a non-idempotent action can create repeated effects if the worker cannot tell what already succeeded.

14
00:01:13.359 --> 00:01:20.849
Use explicit ExampleRetryPolicy settings for delay, maximum attempts, retryable conditions, and failure routing.

15
00:01:20.849 --> 00:01:23.791
Non-idempotent actions require special care.

16
00:01:23.791 --> 00:01:33.889
Payments, state transitions, notifications, exports, report publication, and external API calls may have side effects outside the worker's transaction.

17
00:01:33.889 --> 00:01:42.516
Before retrying, the worker needs a way to determine whether the action already completed or whether it is safe to attempt again.

18
00:01:42.516 --> 00:01:47.933
When that cannot be proven, manual review may be safer than automatic repetition.

19
00:01:47.933 --> 00:01:52.012
Poison messages and dead-letter queues support safe handling.

20
00:01:52.012 --> 00:01:59.635
A message that repeatedly fails validation or processing should not loop forever and starve the rest of the queue.

21
00:01:59.635 --> 00:02:15.417
A dead-letter or review workflow should preserve enough safe context for troubleshooting: ExampleJobId, message type, failure category, timestamp, retry count, and correlation ID, while avoiding sensitive content where it is not needed.

22
00:02:15.417 --> 00:02:21.837
Transaction boundaries, compensation, and recovery planning should be designed before incidents.

23
00:02:21.837 --> 00:02:32.537
Job state transitions should be recorded clearly: received, validated, authorized, processing, completed, failed, dead-lettered, canceled, or manually reviewed.

24
00:02:32.537 --> 00:02:41.163
Those states help operators understand what happened and help auditors distinguish safe retries from repeated unintended effects.
